(Introduction to Intelligent Agents) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(An intelligent agent perceives its environment through sensors and acts on that) Tj T*
(environment through actuators. A rational agent selects the action expected to maximize) Tj T*
(its performance measure, given the percept sequence and available knowledge. Rationality) Tj T*
(is not the same as omniscience: an agent cannot know the actual outcome of every action) Tj T*
(in advance.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The PEAS framework describes a task environment using Performance measure, Environment,) Tj T*
(Actuators, and Sensors. For a robot vacuum, a performance measure can include) Tj T*
(cleanliness and energy use. Its environment is the room. Wheels and suction are) Tj T*
(actuators, while dirt and distance detectors are sensors.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(An environment is fully observable if sensors provide all information relevant to) Tj T*
(choosing an action. In a partially observable environment, the agent has incomplete) Tj T*
(information. An environment is deterministic if the next state is completely determined) Tj T*
(by the current state and the action taken. In a stochastic environment, outcomes involve) Tj T*
(uncertainty.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A simple reflex agent selects actions using condition-action rules based on the current) Tj T*
(percept. It does not retain percept history. A model-based reflex agent maintains) Tj T*
(internal state to track aspects of the environment that are not immediately observable.) Tj T*
(This internal state is updated using percepts and a model of how the world changes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A goal-based agent chooses actions that move it toward a desired goal. It may use search) Tj T*
(and planning to consider future action sequences. A utility-based agent evaluates how) Tj T*
(desirable different outcomes are using a utility function. This helps compare) Tj T*
(alternatives that achieve a goal but differ in quality.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A learning agent improves with experience. Its learning element improves the performance) Tj T*
(element. A critic provides feedback on how well the agent is doing relative to a) Tj T*
(performance standard. A problem generator suggests exploratory actions that may produce) Tj T*
(new and informative experiences.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In a study assistant, lecture files form part of the environment. A controller) Tj T*
(coordinates the workflow. A planner breaks a student goal into steps. An executor) Tj T*
(performs the steps by calling tools. An observer checks whether a result is present and) Tj T*
(well-formed. A critic evaluates the result against lecture content. Memory stores) Tj T*
(session data and previous outputs.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A bounded revision loop limits the number of corrections. For this example, the) Tj T*
(assistant can revise an output at most once. A second review decides whether the) Tj T*
(corrected result is acceptable. This avoids indefinite retries, limits cost, and makes) Tj T*
(behavior easier to explain.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Grounding means basing an answer on the provided source material. Unsupported facts) Tj T*
(should be removed. A second AI review can help identify mistakes, but it does not) Tj T*
(guarantee that an answer is correct. Students should check important details against) Tj T*
ET
